From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Hughes <ryan@heyoodle.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 21:45:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] PCI/ACPI: Mark USB4 tunneled devices removable on every ACPI
 platform

Whether a PCI device is tunneled through a USB4/Thunderbolt port, and so
untrusted and removable, is decided by arch_pci_dev_is_removable(). Only
x86 implements it, although it reads nothing but ACPI: the
"ExternalFacingPort" property of the Root Port and the
"usb4-host-interface" property of the tunneling port.

ACPI arm64 laptops with USB4, such as those built on the NVIDIA N1x,
describe their tunnel Root Ports with the same properties. There the
check is stubbed out, so everything plugged into a dock is trusted: it
keeps the identity domain of a passthrough IOMMU and is never bounced,
and the Thunderbolt core cannot honestly report DMA protection.

Move the check into pci-acpi.c as pci_acpi_dev_is_removable() so every
ACPI platform uses it. The Intel Root Port IDs it falls back to only
match Intel devices, so nothing changes on x86.
---
 arch/x86/pci/acpi.c    | 119 --------------------------------------
 drivers/pci/pci-acpi.c | 127 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 drivers/pci/pci.h      |   5 ++
 drivers/pci/probe.c    |   4 +-
 include/linux/pci.h    |   6 --
 5 files changed, 134 insertions(+), 127 deletions(-)

